WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.370
What do you think?

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:04.190
Would this man make a good doctor?

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.430
What about him?

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.500
Would you trust him with your surgery?

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.320
Most people would probably want to get treated
by one, rather than the other.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:22.150
And most people are likely to be wrong about
that.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.200
When you look at the sun, you sometimes see
it clearly.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.390
But sometimes you’ll see it shining way
bigger than its actual shape.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:36.550
That circle of light that makes it look bigger
is called a halo.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:43.350
The halo effect also happens when a person,
product, or company shines like the sun.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:51.100
Then we don’t see them clearly and associate
all sorts of unrelated qualities to them.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.899
The halo effect was first identified in 1907
by the American psychologist Frederick Wells

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:03.230
and was then studied by the psychologist Edward
Thorndike, who asked flight commanders to

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:09.820
evaluate their officers in various distinct
aspects such as physical appearance, intelligence,

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.080
and leadership.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.910
Examining the results, Thorndike found that
the officers that got high scores for their

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:24.270
psychical qualities, also got rated high on
intelligence and leadership skills - a correlation

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.130
that seemed wrong.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:33.130
It appears that the commanders were unable
to evaluate specific traits independently

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.280
of others.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:42.170
They thought of their officers in broad terms;
either "good" or "bad", and allowed this general

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.950
feeling to influence the specific qualities
they attributed to them.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.910
Some officers profited from their halo.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:56.310
Others were put at a disadvantage by what
is called the horn effect.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:04.430
The halo effect also explains why some teachers
give better looking students higher grades

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:12.099
One study looked at the grades of 4,500 pupils,
who were sorted by volunteers into three groups:

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:16.439
below-average, average, and above-average
looking.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:21.319
Did the better looking people get better grades?

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.870
The researchers then compared students' grades
between classes taken in traditional classrooms

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:32.700
with those taken online where there was little
to no face-to-face interaction.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.480
The researchers later found evidence that
students who were rated as good looking, earned

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:43.170
significantly lower grades in online courses
compared to traditional classrooms where they

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.159
got help from their halo.

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:52.370
Since the physical attributes of good looking
people seem to naturally make them also appear

00:02:52.370 --> 00:03:00.099
intelligent, strong, and trustworthy, here
is one good rule of thumb.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:05.709
If you have an accident and you have to choose
between two equally qualified doctors, ignore

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:09.300
their halo and choose the less handsome one.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:15.299
He might have worked twice as hard to gain
the same reputation and is likely better at

00:03:15.299 --> 00:03:17.319
his job.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:18.319
What do you think?

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:25.330
Do you think it is possible to avoid the halo
effect at work, in school or in public life?

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.430
And what about other credentials that may
signal success?

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:48.569
Please share your thoughts and ideas in the
comments below.

